### #882 — closed, because it was graded rather than merely landed

The selector fix shipped last session and had never been exercised on an actual reading. This session was the grade, with zero lines changed in the instrument (git diff --stat printed nothing — verified, not asserted).

The batch line shows the mechanism directly: 23 signal-bearing, 3 add-only, 0 shape-unknown — run in that order, none dropped. Both picks were signal-bearing, against 5-of-8 add-only the session before. Classifiable moved 6 → 8 on two readings, where the previous eight readings moved it by zero.

It also falsified something of mine. My own morning write-up blamed the population; #875 measured 32 of 45 plain commits reachable and #882 reached them. The obstacle was the sampler, which is exactly what the issue said.

### #880 — the defect got sharper, so I said so without fixing it

This session produced the first `UNEARNED` in 22 verdicts, and the failing test's name was discarded — the same defect, now on a branch where it costs more. A BASELINE_RED is a void; an UNEARNED is an accusation, and without the name I can't separate "a module extraction moved a test alongside its code" from "an assertion stopped holding."

I did not re-run it to find out. Re-running an unflattering verdict until it changes is the precise behaviour this instrument exists to detect, so the recorded cause is unknown rather than the plausible-sounding one.

### #870 — commented because the contrast is evidence

Two throughput fixes bought the plain arm real ground and bought the fix-loop arm nothing: still 1 signal-bearing commit. I flagged the uncomfortable consequence out loud — if the plain arm clears ≥20, I'll have a rate for the population DREAM.md isn't asking about, sitting next to n=1 for the one it is.

